# Version control
.git/
.gitignore

# Not needed to build the Go binary: reference-only source dumps and the
# separate Rust sleep-cycle worker (invoked as a subprocess per ADR-0005,
# not compiled into aisotropyd).
external/
crates/*/target/

# Docs, notes, and non-build metadata
docs/
raw-notes/
spikes/
bench/
*.md

# Editor/OS cruft and local tooling config
.claude/
.agent-tooling/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# Container/build artifacts that should never be baked into the context
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Locally-built binaries dropped in the repo root by `go build ./cmd/X` without
# -o. They are gitignored, so they never reach the repo — but `docker build`
# sends the whole working directory, so without these lines the context carries
# ~380 MB of stale binaries (measured: aisotropyd 177 MB + bitrate-harness
# 178 MB + token-economy 26 MB) that the builder re-compiles from source anyway.
# Mirrors the "Go (producto)" block in .gitignore — keep the two in sync.
/aisotropyd
/bitrate-harness
/skill-import
/embed-bench
/token-economy
/bin/
*.exe
*.out
*.test

# Sibling Go modules and per-language playgrounds. None of them is imported by
# the aisotropyd binary (each arena adapter and example is its OWN module), yet
# together they dominate the context: arenas/ ~3.8 GB (node_modules, venvs,
# per-adapter builds), examples/ ~2.6 GB (each example's own compiled binary),
# .agent-tooling/ ~8.1 GB (agent checkpoints). Excluding them changes nothing
# about what the image contains — the builder compiles ./cmd/aisotropyd from
# the root module only.
arenas/
examples/
test/

# Secrets must never enter the build context — a COPY . . in any builder stage
# would otherwise bake them into an image layer (recoverable even after a later
# stage "removes" them). Belt-and-suspenders with the secret-scanning gate in
# scripts/supply-chain-guard.sh. See docs/deployment/supply-chain.md.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*secret*
